body:
I've found this to fix the problem (which also still happens on master @ eda2a482e9ce82e4056cfffae0ea98c1954605a1). Looks like a long-standing bug? There appears to be a subtle divergence in who should use `set_name` or write to the name directly for a node. I made this change on commit hash a13da4f:

[node.cpp:2865](https://github.com/godotengine/godot/blob/4.7.1-stable/scene/main/node.cpp#L2865): 

```cpp
	if (get_name() != String()) {
		// node->set_name(get_name());
		node->_set_name_nocheck(get_name()); // do this instead 
	}
```

In the node class, the `_duplicate` function uses the `set_name` API whereas `add_child` (which calls `_validate_child_name`) just writes to the name directly like this:

```cpp
p_child->data.name = name;
```

I noticed in `ustring.cpp`, `@` is an invalid character to have in a node name:

[ustring.cpp:5068](https://github.com/godotengine/godot/blob/4.7.1-stable/core/string/ustring.cpp#L5068):

```cpp
static const char32_t invalid_node_name_characters[] = { '.', ':', '@', '/', '\"', UNIQUE_NODE_PREFIX[0], 0 };
```

When you call `validate_node_name` in `ustring.cpp`, which the `_duplicate` function does via the `set_name` function, Godot just changes the `@` to an `_`:

```cpp
		const char32_t *c = invalid_node_name_characters;
		while (*c) {
			if (nn[idx] == *c) {
				nn[idx] = '_';
				break;
			}
			c++;
		}
```

So, our duplicates have `_`'s in their name, while the original nodes made with `add_child` have `@`'s (because they were written directly without going through `set_name`). So that `get_node_or_null` basically never found anything in the Node's children map since the names (acting as the keys) were different.

I see in the commit message for the `set_name` call in `_duplicate` reads the following:

```
commit 0b806ee0fc9097fa7bda7ac0109191c9c5e0a1ac
Author: Juan Linietsky <reduzio@gmail.com>
Date:   Sun Feb 9 22:10:30 2014 -0300

    GODOT IS OPEN SOURCE

diff --git a/scene/main/node.cpp b/scene/main/node.cpp
--- /dev/null
+++ b/scene/main/node.cpp
@@ -0,0 +1199,11 @@
+       for(List<PropertyInfo>::Element *E=plist.front();E;E=E->next()) {
+
+               if (!(E->get().usage&PROPERTY_USAGE_STORAGE))
+                       continue;
+               String name = E->get().name;
+               node->set( name, get(name) );
+
+       }
+
+       node->set_name(get_name());
```

So my guess is that, as is reasonably so, we used `set_name` API to ... set the name! Instead of writing to it directly.

Could someone explain why we have some inconsistencies in how we are setting the names? Sometimes we set the name directly, sometimes we use `set_name` ... I'm new to the source code so missing out on a lot of tribal knowledge here.

EDIT: I can see that from the way things are written the engine might be using those disallowed characters as a means of differentiation between user set names and engine-set names, or something to that effect.

`_duplicate` has been using `set_name` since Godot has been open sourced, and didn't do any name sanitization then. First time this seems to have been introduced in `ustring.cpp` seems to be 6758b6c1317bbdabd04a2c9f8dc16876429b6bfb when I used git pickaxe.

Anyways, with your exact original MRP, I now get the following result with that change:

```
original.single_ref: @Node@3:<Node#36289119751>
original.many_refs.size: 5
  @Node@4:<Node#36305896968> - name: @Node@4
  @Node@5:<Node#36322674185> - name: @Node@5
  @Node@6:<Node#36339451402> - name: @Node@6
  @Node@7:<Node#36356228619> - name: @Node@7
  @Node@8:<Node#36373005836> - name: @Node@8
clone.single_ref: @Node@3:<Node#36406560270>
clone.many_refs.size: 5
  @Node@4:<Node#36423337487> - name: @Node@4 
  @Node@5:<Node#36440114704> - name: @Node@5 
  @Node@6:<Node#36456891921> - name: @Node@6 
  @Node@7:<Node#36473669138> - name: @Node@7 
  @Node@8:<Node#36490446355> - name: @Node@8
```

Original tree is already guaranteed to have unique properties/names, so `_duplicate` doesn't have to check and can accommodate both engine-chosen and user-chosen names. Anyways even without more digging it appears to me this has probably been a long standing bug? In any case I don't see why this shouldn't work. The fix gives intuitive behavior IMO.
